BrailleBridge — Testing Plan

1. Purpose

This document defines the proposed testing procedure for BrailleBridge, a modular refreshable Braille display designed to convert digital text into tactile Braille patterns.

The initial prototype focuses on one six-dot Braille cell. Testing will evaluate the electronic control system, individual pin movement, pattern accuracy, repeatability, usability and repairability.

Current status: Testing plan prepared. Physical prototype testing and performance measurements are pending.

2. Testing Objectives

The objectives are to:

* Verify that supported characters are mapped to the correct six-dot Braille patterns.
* Confirm that each of the six pins can be controlled independently.
* Check that pins reach their intended raised and lowered positions.
* Evaluate the consistency of repeated movements.
* Measure the time required to update a Braille pattern.
* Identify mechanical interference, excessive friction and actuator limitations.
* Assess whether components can be inspected and replaced.
* Document prototype cost and identify opportunities for improvement.

3. Test Environment

The proposed initial test setup includes:

* Arduino Uno R3.
* PCA9685 servo driver.
* Six MG90S micro servos.
* Six-dot Braille pin mechanism.
* Proposed external 5 V power supply.
* Computer with a serial monitor.
* Measuring tools suitable for checking pin movement and dimensions.
* Camera or smartphone for recording observations.

The actual test setup and component specifications must be confirmed before testing begins.

4. Functional Tests

Test 1: Controller and Servo Driver Communication

Objective: Verify communication between the Arduino and the PCA9685 servo driver.

Procedure:

1. Inspect the wiring before powering the circuit.
2. Confirm the SDA and SCL connections.
3. Check the configured I2C address.
4. Run an appropriate communication test.
5. Record whether the controller communicates successfully with the driver.

Measurements: Communication success or failure, detected I2C address and any error messages.

Status: Pending.

Test 2: Individual Pin Actuation

Objective: Verify independent movement of all six Braille pins.

Procedure:

1. Test the actuator associated with the first pin.
2. Observe whether the intended pin moves.
3. Repeat for the remaining five pins.
4. Check for unwanted movement of neighbouring pins.
5. Record any binding, slipping or abnormal noise.

Measurements: Number of pins operating correctly out of six, movement consistency and observed interference.

Status: Pending.

Test 3: Raised and Lowered Pin States

Objective: Determine whether each pin can reliably reach its intended raised and lowered positions.

Procedure:

1. Command a pin to its proposed raised position.
2. Measure or observe the resulting position.
3. Command it to its lowered position.
4. Repeat the procedure for all six pins.
5. Record the measured travel and any failures.

Measurements: Pin travel, consistency between states and any mechanical obstruction.

Status: Pending.

Test 4: Braille Pattern Accuracy

Objective: Check whether the software commands the correct pin combination for each supported character.

Procedure:

1. Select a test character.
2. Determine its expected six-dot pattern using the project’s chosen Braille mapping reference.
3. Enter the character through the supported input method.
4. Record the commanded pin states.
5. Compare the commands with the expected pattern.
6. Repeat with additional supported characters.

Measurements: Number of correct patterns divided by the total number of patterns tested.

Calculation:

Pattern accuracy (%) = (Correct patterns / Total patterns tested) × 100

Software mapping accuracy and physical actuation accuracy should be recorded separately.

Status: Pending.

Test 5: Repeated Operation

Objective: Evaluate whether the mechanism produces consistent pin states over repeated refresh cycles.

Procedure:

1. Select a repeatable sequence of test patterns.
2. Run the sequence multiple times.
3. Observe every pin during the sequence.
4. Record missed movements, inconsistent positions, jamming or unusual noise.
5. Inspect the mechanism for visible wear.

Measurements: Number of successful cycles, number of observed failures and any change in pin movement.

The number of cycles will be decided before the test and recorded in the results.

Status: Pending.

Test 6: Refresh Time

Objective: Measure the time required to update the display from one pattern to another.

Procedure:

1. Choose a starting pattern and a different target pattern.
2. Record the start of the update command.
3. Measure the time until all pins reach their intended states.
4. Repeat with different patterns.
5. Calculate the average measured refresh time.

Measurements: Individual refresh times, average refresh time and any incomplete updates.

The timing method and test conditions must be documented so that results can be reproduced.

Status: Pending.

Test 7: Navigation and Input

Objective: Verify the supported text-input and navigation functions.

Procedure:

1. Enter a short test string using the implemented input method.
2. Check whether supported characters are processed correctly.
3. Test the Previous and Next functions if implemented.
4. Test Home and Reset if implemented.
5. Record incorrect output, missed commands or unexpected behaviour.

Measurements: Successful operations, failed operations and observed software errors.

Only functions implemented in the actual firmware should be tested.

Status: Pending.

Test 8: Power and Electrical Behaviour

Objective: Evaluate the electrical system under the intended operating conditions.

Procedure:

1. Verify supply voltage and polarity before connecting the circuit.
2. Check that the power supply and wiring are suitable for the connected servos.
3. Operate the mechanism using representative test patterns.
4. Monitor supply voltage and current where suitable instruments are available.
5. Record resets, unstable movement, excessive heating or other abnormal behaviour.

Measurements: Supply voltage, current under recorded test conditions and observed electrical issues.

The servo power supply must be designed for the expected combined load. A proposed power-supply rating is not proof that the completed system is electrically safe.

Status: Pending.

Test 9: Maintenance and Repairability

Objective: Evaluate how easily an individual component can be inspected and replaced.

Procedure:

1. Identify a component selected for maintenance.
2. Record the tools and steps required to access it.
3. Remove and reinstall or replace the component where practical.
4. Check whether the assembly can be restored without damaging other parts.
5. Document the time required and any difficulties.

Measurements: Maintenance time, tools required, components disturbed and any damage observed.

Status: Pending.

5. Initial Success Criteria

The following are proposed targets or evaluation goals, not achieved results.

Parameter	Proposed Evaluation
Braille mapping	Target at least 99% correct software pattern generation during controlled testing
Independent actuation	All six pins should respond to their intended commands
Pin movement	Raised and lowered states should be distinguishable and repeatable
Refresh time	Measure and report actual results; establish a target after initial trials
Repeatability	Record successful cycles and all observed failures
Repairability	Demonstrate component access and document replacement steps
Prototype cost	Record actual component and manufacturing costs
Reliability	Identify failure modes and document corrective actions

Software pattern-generation accuracy does not establish physical display accuracy. The two must be measured independently.

6. Results Record

After each test, record the following information:

Field	Details
Test ID	Unique test number
Test date	Date performed
Hardware configuration	Components and versions used
Procedure	Steps followed
Expected result	Intended behaviour
Actual result	Observed behaviour
Measurements	Numerical readings where available
Pass/Fail	Based on the predefined criterion
Evidence	Photograph, video, log or measurement record
Issues	Errors or unexpected behaviour
Corrective action	Changes planned or completed

No test should be marked as passed without recorded evidence supporting the result.

7. Evidence to Include in the Repository

The following evidence will be added as it becomes available:

* Photographs of the assembled prototype.
* Videos showing individual pin movement.
* Photographs or screenshots of the wiring.
* Serial monitor logs.
* Braille pattern test results.
* Pin-travel and refresh-time measurements.
* Repeated-operation results.
* CAD drawings and mechanical revisions.
* Component cost records.
* A record of problems identified and improvements made.

Personal information should not be included in publicly shared test evidence.

8. Risk Identification

Potential issues to investigate include:

* Pins sticking because of friction or misalignment.
* Servo torque or travel being insufficient for the mechanism.
* Linkages interfering with neighbouring components.
* Inconsistent pin heights.
* Excessive current draw when multiple servos move.
* Controller resets caused by power instability.
* Mechanical wear during repeated operation.
* Incorrect character-to-pattern mapping.

These are potential risks to investigate, not confirmed failures.

9. Future Testing

After the single-cell mechanism has been validated, testing can be extended to a four-cell, 24-pin configuration.

Future testing may evaluate:

* Coordination between multiple cells.
* Longer text sequences.
* More extensive repeated-operation testing.
* Overall system dimensions and weight.
* Power consumption during representative operation.
* Assembly time and maintenance effort.
* Usability feedback from people familiar with Braille.

The expanded configuration remains a stretch goal until the initial cell is demonstrated.

10. Conclusion

This plan establishes a structured method for evaluating BrailleBridge’s software, electronics and mechanical design.

Testing will begin with basic communication and individual pin movement, followed by pattern accuracy, repeated operation, refresh-time measurement and maintenance evaluation.

Results will be recorded transparently, with proposed targets clearly distinguished from measured performance. The test plan will be updated as the design develops and new evidence becomes available.

